From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 11 Jul 2026 15:24:35 -0700
Subject: [RFC net-next v1 0/1] net: flow_dissector: adaptive auto-enable for the fast-path gates

G'day,

This RFC builds on "net: flow_dissector: opt-in byte-identical fast
paths for common shapes":

  https://lore.kernel.org/netdev/SERIES-A-LORE-LINK/

That series leaves the operator seven per-shape policy decisions. The
per-shape counters it adds (/proc/net/flow_dissector_stats) contain
exactly the signal needed to make those decisions, so the obvious
question is whether the kernel should make them itself. This RFC is
that experiment: a packet-window controller that manages the
byte-identical gates automatically, making "turn on the fast path" one
knob instead of seven. It is an RFC because auto-tuning static keys
from a kworker is a policy mechanism netdev may simply not want --
the fast-path series stands on its own without it.

Shape: every ~auto_window_packets dissects (default 1M; the single
tuning knob), a worker compares each shape's windowed eligible
fraction against its measured break-even and flips the gate, with
hysteresis (+10pp on, -5pp off), a 3-window dwell, and a >=1s
per-shape flip-rate cap so a crafted burst cannot force static-key
patch storms. Decisions use window deltas, never cumulative counters
(a since-boot ratio asymptotically freezes). The hot path pays one
not-taken static branch with auto off -- the same discipline as the
gates themselves -- and one per-cpu increment plus a cached-threshold
compare with it on (no division on the packet path); the counter
summation and the static_branch flips (text_poke, which can sleep)
run in the worker, serialised against the sysctl writers with
cancel_work_sync(). Individual gates can be pinned by hand while auto
runs (net.flow_dissector.auto_exclude, default mpls); shape
management is a whitelist, so the descent gates from the companion
series can never become auto-managed. One documented interaction: an
attached netns BPF flow dissector zeroes the controller's input (its
dissects are deliberately not counted), so auto will park the gates
off -- consistent, since they are unreachable behind the program.

The per-shape policy is a pure function, unit-tested in the KUnit
suite the fast-path series added: dwell requirement, both margin
boundaries, dwell reset on a dip, hysteresis deadband, the
low-break-even clamp, and dwell saturation (a rate-capped flip stays
pending rather than wrapping away). mpls is excluded by default
(its break-even is 60-70%: a net loss unless mpls dominates), and the
UDP-tunnel descent gates are structurally unmanageable -- enabling
them changes hashing and must stay a deliberate operator action.

Open questions for the list -- the first is the make-or-break, and we
are asking it deliberately rather than assuming the answer:

  1. Is an in-kernel controller wanted at all? Everything it reads
     (flow_dissector_stats) and writes (the per-shape sysctls) is
     already exposed, so userspace can do this today; the equivalent
     agent is about a dozen lines:

       #!/bin/sh
       # flow-dissector auto-enable, userspace edition
       while sleep 10; do
           awk 'NR>1 { frac = $4; gate = "/proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/" $1
                       thresh[$1] > 0 || thresh[$1] = 25
                       print (frac > thresh[$1]) ? 1 : 0 > gate }'                /proc/net/flow_dissector_stats
       done

     (No hysteresis, dwell, or flip caps -- the in-kernel controller
     exists because doing this well needs those, packet-proportional
     windows rather than a timer, and no daemon dependency. Whether
     that clears netdev's bar for in-kernel policy is exactly the
     question.)
  2. The break-even table is measured offline (conservative in-order
     row) and currently hard-coded. Runtime-tunable per shape is the
     obvious next step; self-measurement a possible follow-up. Is a
     hard-coded conservative table acceptable for an initial merge?
  3. The gates are global static keys patching shared text, so the
     controller is host-wide by construction (the per-netns escape
     hatch is the netns BPF flow dissector, which takes precedence).
     Should the controller sit behind a CONFIG_ option so distros can
     opt out at build time?

Known-deferred items, acknowledged rather than hidden: worker-level
KUnit tests (the pure policy function is unit-tested; the worker
plumbing -- rate-cap timing, dwell preservation across capped flips,
pinned-gate non-interference -- is not yet) and the runtime-tunable
break-even table from question 2.

Testing: KUnit 54/54 at the RFC tip (the fast-path suite + the policy
case); per-patch W=1, sparse and smatch clean; checkpatch --strict
clean. Supporting data (break-even derivation, controller design
notes) in the author's XDP2 fork:
https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2 , under perf-results/ .

On tooling: as with the fast-path series, developed with substantial
LLM assistance (Claude, Anthropic), human-reviewed and human-measured;
the patch carries an Assisted-by: trailer in the
Documentation/process/coding-assistants.rst format.

